  ("Psychologists have long observed that repeated, unreinforced" 0.085 0.48411 0.37511 0.3914 0.3914 0.01629 0.00437 0.2712 0.0875 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("exposure results in an increase in positive affect toward a stimu-" 0.06958 0.48485 0.38899 0.40528 0.40528 0.01629 0.00437 0.3383 0.0605 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("lus (Fechner, 1876; Maslow, 1937). A number of early studies" 0.06958 0.48561 0.40287 0.41916 0.41916 0.01629 0.00437 0.3242 0.0189 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("in this area (e.g., Meyer, 1903; Mpore & Gilliland, 1924; Wash-" 0.06958 0.48526 0.41706 0.43335 0.43335 0.01629 0.00437 0.4321 0.0126 "Times-Roman" 0.0123 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("burn, Child, & Abel, 1927) investigated the effects of repeated" 0.06958 0.48494 0.43126 0.44754 0.44754 0.01629 0.00427 0.3128 0.0367 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("exposures on affect ratings of music, generally finding more" 0.07 0.4855 0.44514 0.46143 0.46143 0.01629 0.00437 0.2662 0.0653 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("positive ratings with increasing familiarity. Many of these ex-" 0.06958 0.48525 0.45902 0.47531 0.47531 0.01629 0.00437 0.3477 0.0507 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("periments were methodologically unsound, however (i.e., in-" 0.06958 0.48526 0.4729 0.48919 0.48919 0.01629 0.00437 0.3082 0.0654 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("volved improper control conditions or groups or no controls at" 0.07 0.48496 0.48709 0.50338 0.50338 0.01629 0.00437 0.2759 0.0433 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("all), and conclusions based on their results are somewhat tenu-" 0.06958 0.48484 0.50097 0.51726 0.51726 0.01629 0.00437 0.2976 0.0257 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("ous. Additional indirect evidence for the hypothesis that in-" 0.07 0.48567 0.51516 0.53145 0.53145 0.01629 0.00437 0.2413 0.0269 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("creased exposure leads to more positive affect toward a stimulus" 0.07 0.48603 0.52935 0.54564 0.54564 0.01629 0.00437 0.3561 0.0478 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("came from studies of social interaction (e.g., Festinger, 1951;" 0.07 0.4861 0.54293 0.55922 0.55922 0.01629 0.00437 0.2807 0.0355 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("Homans, 1961;Newcomb, 1963). Although these investigations" 0.07042 0.48562 0.55712 0.57341 0.57341 0.01629 0.00437 0.3547 0.0596 "Times-Roman" 0.0127 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("consistently demonstrated that interaction enhances the atti-" 0.07 0.48568 0.57131 0.5876 0.5876 0.01629 0.00437 0.2579 0.0767 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("tudes of participants toward each other, it is impossible to tell" 0.06958 0.48493 0.58519 0.60148 0.60148 0.01629 0.00437 0.3039 0.0351 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("to what degree this is due to mere exposure, to reinforcement" 0.07 0.48537 0.59938 0.61567 0.61567 0.01629 0.00437 0.3153 0.0134 "Times-Roman" 0.0127 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("derived from social interaction, or to some combination of these" 0.07042 0.48508 0.61326 0.62955 0.62955 0.01629 0.00437 0.3461 0.0479 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("(and other) variables." 0.07042 0.21041 0.62745 0.64374 0.64374 0.01629 0.00437 0.2442 0.0295 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("With the publication of Zajonc's (1968) monograph on the" 0.08583 0.4855 0.64133 0.65762 0.65762 0.01629 0.00434 0.3130 0.0334 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("\"Attitudinal Effects of Mere Exposure,\" the exposure effect be-" 0.07042 0.48608 0.65521 0.6715 0.6715 0.01629 0.00425 0.3204 0.0820 "Times-Roman" 0.0124 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("came a topic of investigation in mainstream psychology. Zajonc" 0.07042 0.48948 0.6694 0.68569 0.68569 0.01629 0.00437 0.3536 0.0348 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("(1968) defined the exposure effect as the observation that \"mere" 0.07042 0.48553 0.68329 0.69958 0.69958 0.01629 0.00433 0.3058 0.0436 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("repeated exposure of the individual to a stimulus is a sufficient" 0.07083 0.48538 0.69748 0.71377 0.71377 0.01629 0.00439 0.3031 0.0552 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("condition for the enhancement of his attitude toward it. By" 0.07083 0.48551 0.71167 0.72796 0.72796 0.01629 0.0043 0.2809 0.0622 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("'mere exposure' is meant a condition which just makes the given" 0.07042 0.48466 0.72555 0.74184 0.74184 0.01629 0.00437 0.4233 0.0380 "Times-Roman" 0.0129 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("stimulus accessible to the individual's perception\" (p. 1). Za-" 0.07042 0.48568 0.73974 0.75603 0.75603 0.01629 0.00442 0.2889 0.0573 "Times-Roman" 0.0125 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("jonc thus suggested that simple, unreinforced exposure leads" 0.06875 0.48603 0.75362 0.76991 0.76991 0.01629 0.00437 0.2452 0.0294 "Times-Roman" 0.0126 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("I would like to thank Nancy E. Cantor, Paul R. D'Agostino, Mary A." 0.08417 0.48519 0.79816 0.81274 0.81274 0.01457 0.00391 0.4426 0.0049 "Times-Roman" 0.0109 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
  ("Languirand, Thane S. Pittman and two anonymous reviewers for their" 0.07083 0.48365 0.81081 0.82538 0.82538 0.01457 0.00386 0.3440 0.0716 "Times-Roman" 0.0111 nil nil 0 "Times-Roman" nil)
